  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Axis Peak</strong> rises to 5,161 feet in Alaska. The Axis Peak Trail covers 15.4 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 132 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=60.47652,-149.47163\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">60.47652, -149.47163</a> (tap for driving directions). Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Cecil Rhode Mountain, Andy Simons Mountain and Race Point \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
